From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 19:37:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 152/190] The first D: open runs the sequence that works, not a
 paraphrase of it

Confirmed again today, on this build's hardware run: CDTEST brings D:
up at round 3, every time, and nothing else ever has. So the first
touch of the disc now does literally what the rounds do -- arm the
timeshare. Our VBL goes silent first (cdd_cmd_on = 0; the silence is
part of the sequence), the firmware gets the whole read: DRV_INIT,
then ROMREADN, a real play command at a real target. Its sector
delivery fails under our environment, stage 3 every time, but it
leaves the drive spun up, positioned and streaming, and after six
failures cd_fill_bios disarms itself and our own reads take over --
which is exactly the T1-through-T3 arc.

The deleted "wake" was my paraphrase of that sequence: DRV_INIT alone,
no ROMREADN, no silence first. It never once worked on hardware.
Approximating a working sequence instead of running it cost four
builds.

Also fixed before shipping: the edit initially left a duplicated brace
that closed cd_read_block early -- caught by -Wunused-variable before
it ever reached a build that was sent anywhere.

What this does not fix, named so it stays in view: Doom-speed needs the
firmware's read to SUCCEED under the timeshare, not merely to warm the
drive up for ours. The loader's subwake proves the firmware can read
this disc on this console -- letter R, every boot. Under the
timeshare's partial environment the same read starves at stage 3. That
one gap is the difference between "works after ~20 seconds, once" and
"snappy, accurate, repeatable".
---
 bios/scdapi.h |  2 +-
 bios/segacd.c | 84 +++++++++++++++++++--------------------------------
 2 files changed, 32 insertions(+), 54 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/scdapi.h b/bios/scdapi.h
index 7534f06..f723c1f 100644
--- a/bios/scdapi.h
+++ b/bios/scdapi.h
@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@
 #define SCDAPI_H
 
 #define SCD_COOKIE   0x53674344L        /* 'SgCD' */
-#define SCD_VERSION  20
+#define SCD_VERSION  21
 
 /* Everything the disc diagnostic prints, in one go. Taken in a single
  * call rather than twenty accessors so that the numbers in one report
diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 54c51b5..205a666 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -3047,61 +3047,39 @@ static BOOL cd_read_block(ULONG block, UBYTE *buf)
      * moment -- one drive, one master. */
     if (!cd_ts_decided && !cd_booting) {
         cd_ts_decided = 1;
-        if (cd_ts_want && segacd_bios_available()) {
+        /* First touch of the disc: run the sequence that works, not an
+         * approximation of it.
+         *
+         * The one thing that has ever brought D: up on this console is
+         * CDTEST's rounds, and what they do is arm the timeshare: our
+         * VBL stops commanding the drive (cdd_cmd_on = 0, and the
+         * silence is part of it), and the firmware gets the whole read
+         * -- DRV_INIT, then ROMREADN, a real play command at a real
+         * target. Its sector delivery fails in our environment, stage
+         * 3 every time, but it leaves the drive spun up, positioned
+         * and streaming; after six failures cd_fill_bios disarms
+         * itself, our commander comes back, and our own reads work.
+         * That is the T1-through-T3 arc, and the user has confirmed it
+         * end to end more times than either of us enjoyed.
+         *
+         * The previous "wake" here ran DRV_INIT alone -- no ROMREADN,
+         * no silence first -- and it never once worked on hardware. So
+         * no more paraphrase: arm the machinery CDTEST arms, let the
+         * existing fallback do what it demonstrably does, and D:'s
+         * first open costs what the diagnostic's rounds cost instead
+         * of needing the diagnostic.
+         *
+         * The remaining gap to Doom-speed is real and it is one bug,
+         * not this one: the firmware CAN read this disc on this
+         * console -- the loader's subwake proves it every boot, letter
+         * R -- but under the timeshare's partial environment its read
+         * starves at stage 3. Make that succeed and the fallback never
+         * fires, and the first open is as fast as every other. */
+        if (segacd_bios_available()) {
             cd_ts = 1;
             cd_ts_cmdsave = cdd_cmd_on;
-            cdd_cmd_on = 0;     /* our VBL stops talking to the drive */
-        } else if (!cd_booting && segacd_bios_available()) {
-            /* Otherwise: let the firmware wake the drive, once, and
-             * then read it ourselves.
-             *
-             * This is the shape the console arrived at by hand and it
-             * took three rounds of a diagnostic to see it. Rounds 1 and
-             * 2 handed the disc to the parked firmware with DRV_INIT
-             * on; round 3 took it back and read the filesystem with
-             * this driver -- Getbpb, Dfree, Fsfirst, Fopen, Fread, all
-             * of them, on the first try. Round 3 alone does nothing,
-             * because nothing else on a normal boot ever calls
-             * DRV_INIT, and this drive does not leave its
-             * no-information state without it. So D: has only ever been
-             * readable after a person launched CDTEST.
-             *
-             * Doom does the same thing and says so plainly: three
-             * DRV_INIT sites in its sub program, one of them the second
-             * instruction of its bring-up. The note in this file
-             * claiming no Sega read path calls DRV_INIT was wrong.
-             *
-             * A count of zero asks segacd_bios_read for the wake and
-             * not the read. It costs one visit -- about two seconds on
-             * the console, measured between diagnostic rounds -- and it
-             * happens on the first block anyone asks for, which is the
-             * boot probe, so the drive is awake before disk_init_all
-             * decides whether D: has a filesystem. */
-            /* Five seconds, not twenty.
-             *
-             * A firmware visit that includes a successful DRV_INIT
-             * costs about 174 of the diagnostic's ticks on the console
-             * -- under three seconds -- so five is generous and twenty
-             * is a tax on an empty tray. The cartridge loader already
-             * spends up to twenty of its own waiting for the drive
-             * before EmuTOS starts, and paying it twice for a machine
-             * with nothing in the tray is not a trade worth making for
-             * headroom nothing has ever used.
-             *
-             * Put back afterwards, because a read that genuinely needs
-             * the drive spun up from cold is a different question from
-             * a boot probe. */
-            /* The loader's own budget for this call, and no less.
-             *
-             * A previous version cut it to 300 frames on the grounds
-             * that a firmware visit costs about 174 of the diagnostic's
-             * ticks. That number came from the gap between two rounds,
-             * only one of which ran DRV_INIT, so it was not a
-             * measurement of DRV_INIT at all. boot/m1emu.S allows
-             * twenty seconds for exactly this call and the drive
-             * answers inside it on every boot; there is no honest
-             * reading that says less will do. */
-            cd_woke = (UBYTE)(segacd_bios_read(0UL, 0) == 0 ? 1 : 2);
+            cdd_cmd_on = 0;
+            cd_woke = 1;
         }
     }
 
